t=para(t,'1.1 Finiteness and the restricted dual.',r'''1.1 Finite rank-one reduction. For homogeneous $x$, choose $N\ge1$ with $E_i^Nx=0$ and put $W_x:=\operatorname{span}\{F_i^{(a)}E_i^bx:0\le b<N,\ a\ge0\}$. Local nilpotence of $F_i$ on each of the finitely many $E_i^bx$ makes this span finite. The divided commutation formula of [F1] proves $E_i$ preserves it, while $F_i$ and $K_i^{\pm1}$ preserve it directly. Thus $W_x$ is a finite-dimensional type-one $A_i$-module, with $E_i,F_i$ nilpotent and $K_i$ diagonalizable; its labels lie on the single line through $\mathrm{wt}(x)$. A general vector has finite label support, so it too lies in a finite sum of such modules. [F1, F2, algebra]''')
t=para(t,'1.2 Maximal vectors.',r'''1.2 Finite rank-one strings, proved locally. In a finite-dimensional type-one $A_i$-module, a nonzero $E_i$-highest eigenvector $y$ of integer weight $a$ has a minimal $t\ge1$ with $F_i^{(t)}y=0$. Applying $E_i$ and [F1] gives $[a-t+1]_iF_i^{(t-1)}y=0$, hence $t=a+1$ and $a\ge0$; the positions $F_i^{(n)}y$, $0\le n\le a$, are nonzero and have distinct $K_i$-eigenvalues. Each simple composition factor has such a vector (take an eigencomponent of a last nonzero $E_i$-iterate), whose string is a nonzero submodule and therefore the entire factor. Put $C:=F_iE_i+(q_iK_i+q_i^{-1}K_i^{-1})/(q_i-q_i^{-1})^2$. The relations of [F1] give $[C,E_i]=[C,F_i]=[C,K_i]=0$, and $C$ acts on a highest string of weight $a$ by $c_a=(q_i^{a+1}+q_i^{-a-1})/(q_i-q_i^{-1})^2$. These scalars are distinct for $a\ge0$: equality factors as $(q_i^{a+1}-q_i^{b+1})(1-q_i^{-a-b-2})=0$, forcing $a=b$. Split a finite module into its generalized $C$-eigenspaces. In the $c_a$-block all composition factors are strings of highest weight $a$, so if their number is $k$, the $K_i$-weight spaces $a,a-2,\ldots,-a$ all have dimension $k$ and there are no other weights. Its top space is killed by $E_i$; the maps $F_i^{(n)}$ on that space are injective for $n\le a$ by the first endpoint argument and vanish for $n>a$. Their images therefore exhaust the block's distinct weight spaces. This proves the finite string decomposition without assuming rank-one complete reducibility. Applied to $W_x$, the tops and positions have the chosen labels: on its single root line, the $K_i$-eigenvalue determines the label since $\langle\alpha_i,h_i\rangle=2$. [step 1.1, F1, F2, algebra]''')
